{% comment %}
  sections/search-drawer.liquid

  The search overlay, in both of its shapes.

  Theme settings › Search › Layout picks between them:

    modal   A centred card. The field sits in a bar across the top and the body
            splits into a column of recent searches and tags beside the results.
    drawer  A panel off the inline-end edge, single column, field under the
            title. The same content, stacked.

  One element, one component, one set of markup. A second section for the modal
  would mean every future fix to the field, the chips or the result rows had to
  be made twice — which is exactly how the two drift apart.

  Focus goes to the field on open rather than to the close button — the one
  exception to the drawer default, because opening search has exactly one
  purpose and a customer who has to Tab to the field will assume it is broken.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign layout = settings.predictive_search_layout | default: 'modal'
-%}

<search-drawer
  id="SearchDrawer"
  class="search-drawer drawer search-drawer--{{ layout }}"
  data-layout="{{ layout }}"
  {%- comment -%}
    The modal is anchored to the top of the viewport rather than centred with
    `align-items: center`: the field has to stay near the header the customer
    just clicked, and a panel that grows downward as results arrive must not
    push its own field up the screen while they read it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if layout == 'drawer' -%}
    data-placement="end"
  {%- else -%}
    data-placement="top"
  {%- endif -%}
  data-overlay-type="search"
>
  <dialog class="drawer__dialog" data-ref="dialog" aria-label="{{ 'search.title' | t }}">
    <div class="drawer__panel search-panel" data-ref="panel">
      {% render 'predictive-search',
         id: 'SearchDrawerInput',
         variant: 'overlay',
         layout: layout,
         close: true %}
    </div>
  </dialog>
</search-drawer>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:sections.search_drawer.name",
  "settings": []
}
{% endschema %}
